WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.313
[MUSIC]

00:00:02.313 --> 00:00:03.423
I'm Rahul Pandey.

00:00:03.423 --> 00:00:05.345
I run my operations out of India.

00:00:05.345 --> 00:00:07.970
I'm a founder CEO of an enterprise

00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:09.876
SAAS company, Bonzai.

00:00:09.876 --> 00:00:11.659
One of the biggest challenges

00:00:11.659 --> 00:00:13.227
brands are going to have is to

00:00:13.227 --> 00:00:15.540
scale their creative content.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:17.713
That was a point when I started to

00:00:17.713 --> 00:00:19.726
think, how might we take this

00:00:19.726 --> 00:00:21.980
challenge and give us solution to

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:23.602
a technology to scale.

00:00:23.602 --> 00:00:25.490
Our customers are across Asia.

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:27.035
We are in Australia, New Zealand,

00:00:27.035 --> 00:00:27.756
Singapore, and

00:00:27.756 --> 00:00:29.620
the other parts of Southeast Asia.

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:31.487
That's the thing which really

00:00:31.487 --> 00:00:33.423
motivates me, drives me to work

00:00:33.423 --> 00:00:35.288
with this incredible team to go

00:00:35.288 --> 00:00:37.513
along and build something awesome,

00:00:37.513 --> 00:00:39.180
and build it for the world.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:39.741
This is my

00:00:39.741 --> 00:00:41.786
total engagement with Stanford.

00:00:41.786 --> 00:00:44.298
The learning in SEP is multi-fold,

00:00:44.298 --> 00:00:45.999
at an individual level,

00:00:45.999 --> 00:00:47.944
at organization level, and

00:00:47.944 --> 00:00:49.321
the society level,

00:00:49.321 --> 00:00:52.050
I learned a lot about leadership.

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:53.907
I learned a lot about how do you

00:00:53.907 --> 00:00:55.765
become a successful leader,

00:00:55.765 --> 00:00:57.258
leading a global team.

00:00:57.258 --> 00:00:58.945
How do you work across different

00:00:58.945 --> 00:00:59.780
culture?

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:01.741
How do you use different frameworks

00:01:01.741 --> 00:01:03.178
to drive the efficiency,

00:01:03.178 --> 00:01:04.210
innovation?

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:06.197
That's been an amazing learning at

00:01:06.197 --> 00:01:07.658
the organization level.

00:01:07.658 --> 00:01:09.310
This is a process of unlearning.

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:11.292
And I realized there are many areas

00:01:11.292 --> 00:01:13.068
where you need to re-look into

00:01:13.068 --> 00:01:15.370
process, people, thinking.

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:17.362
I think each of the faculty members

00:01:17.362 --> 00:01:18.949
are masters in their area.

00:01:18.949 --> 00:01:20.925
So to me, every professor I met,

00:01:20.925 --> 00:01:22.483
they are my superheroes.

00:01:22.483 --> 00:01:24.789
They are inspiring me to go and

00:01:24.789 --> 00:01:26.270
change the world.

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:28.825
I feel more confident as a leader,

00:01:28.825 --> 00:01:30.675
I feel more energetic and

00:01:30.675 --> 00:01:32.084
charged up to go and

00:01:32.084 --> 00:01:33.947
get Bonzai to the world.

00:01:33.947 --> 00:01:34.447
[MUSIC]

